  "semantic_claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "majority-ground",
      "proposition": "The majority holds that the FAA requires enforcing the arbitration agreement as to the Section 362(k) damages claim because nothing in the Bankruptcy Code's text (Section 362(k), or the core-proceeding designation in 28 U.S.C. 157) supplies the clear and manifest congressional command Epic Systems and CompuCredit demand; it rejects the Fourth Circuit's McMahon-derived 'inherent conflict with the Code's underlying purposes' inquiry as a basis for displacing the FAA, endorsing the Second Circuit's result in MBNA v. Hill."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "ground-breadth",
      "proposition": "The ground is categorical rather than case-specific: the Court announces that a bankruptcy court has no discretion to refuse enforcement of a valid arbitration agreement over a federal statutory claim on the basis of generalized Bankruptcy Code policies or core-proceeding status, reaching beyond Section 362(k) and these debtors, while expressly reserving whether a bankruptcy court's own Section 105(a) contempt power over violations of its stay may be kept from arbitration."
    }
  ],
